<!-- cell: title-page id="c66e6bcb-c806-4441-ac32-f22982296d22" title="The Forty-Fourth Lodger" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="c31b3478-39d7-46a6-b6a2-eb97f822ebf7" -->

Mrs. Agnes Pell had let the back bedroom at 9 Garnet Street to forty-three lodgers since her husband died, and she had a rule for each of them: rent on Fridays, no cooking in the room, and conversation in the kitchen after supper, because a house that is never talked in begins to forget it is a house.

The forty-fourth lodger came in November, in the rain, with no luggage at all. He was a thin, courteous man in a dark coat who gave his name as Mr. Lark and paid a month in advance with coins so old the bank could not say what they were worth.

He did not eat. Every evening she put a plate in front of him, and every evening he thanked her and left it untouched. The kitchen grew cold when he sat in it, and the hall clock, which had kept time since 1952, stopped whenever he passed it.

But he talked, and more than that, he listened. He asked about her husband, Wilf, and the shop they had kept, and her sister in Leeds who no longer wrote, and he listened as if every word were worth keeping. No one had listened to Agnes like that for eleven years.

On the first Friday in December, as she cleared his untouched plate, he asked her gently, "Are you ready, Mrs. Pell?" She thought he meant the rent, and laughed, and said not yet.

He asked again the next Friday, and the one after that, always kindly, never pressing, and she began to understand that he was not asking about the rent. She began to notice how tired she was, how the stairs had become a mountain, how her hands looked like paper in the lamplight.

On Christmas Eve she set two places as usual and sat down across from him, and looked for a long time at the empty chair where Wilf used to sit.

"Are you ready, Mrs. Pell?" "Yes," said Agnes. "I think I am."

The hall clock stopped.

It was the new year before the neighbours noticed the milk collecting on the step. They found her in her chair at the kitchen table, two places set, her face peaceful. Upstairs the back bedroom was locked, and when the police opened it, the dust on the bed and the floor lay thick and unbroken, as if no one had slept there for years.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="d56af899-b82b-4b3e-af18-bb790dbd0054" title="Agnes Pell" words="0-57, 186-206, 228-239, 268-291, 292-321, 327-333, 353-368" -->

A widowed landlady, kind and orderly, whose rules keep a house alive. She has gone unheard for eleven years, is more tired than she admits, and in the end chooses to go.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="28c95e55-c4aa-4c56-aefa-908680889ef2" title="Mr. Lark" words="58-107, 108-131, 156-206, 207-227, 240-267, 322-326, 369-401" -->

The forty-fourth lodger: courteous, patient, without luggage or appetite. He listens as no one has, and keeps asking whether she is ready. He is death, though the story never says so.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="b9a12e52-d626-4262-8bfb-0bc6eb3eb317" title="9 Garnet Street" words="0-57, 132-155, 334-337, 338-352, 369-401" -->

Agnes's boarding house: the back bedroom, the kitchen where she insists on conversation, the hall clock that has kept time since 1952, and the step where the milk collects.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="0856d9f8-6f24-4dc3-a19c-4c240362df32" title="Loneliness" words="43-57, 156-206, 292-321, 338-352" -->

Agnes's solitude since her husband died: the sister who no longer writes, the empty chair, and the neighbours who notice only the milk.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="061cf6c3-377e-4b4d-becb-d5a22b18c049" title="Death" words="72-107, 108-155, 207-227, 240-291, 322-333, 334-337, 353-368" -->

Death come gently for Agnes, never named: the stopped clock, the cold, the untouched food, the ancient coins and the question 'Are you ready?'

<!-- cell: plot id="23187b0f-2b5f-4336-a138-8839b36ce952" title="The forty-fourth lodger" nodes="n1:58-71, n2:108-155, n3:207-239, n4:240-267, n5:292-321, n6:322-333, n7:334-337, n8:353-401" -->

A strange lodger arrives, never eats, and keeps asking whether Agnes is ready; she comes to understand the question and, on Christmas Eve, says yes; she is found dead in her chair, and his room has not been slept in for years.
